  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 137 PM CDT Thu Aug 20 2026\n\nThe main story for this afternoon and evening will be \nthunderstorm chances. As of the early afternoon, a broad swath \nof clouds with embedded showers and storms cover much of the \nNorthland with some pockets of clearing. The current \nshowers/storms are associated with the warm sector of a low \npressure system, which is centered near the Canadian Border over\nnorthwest/north-central Minnesota. The low is expected to move \nsoutheast this afternoon, and there is an area of clearing out \nnear Upper/Lower Red Lake that could be a focus for additional \nthunderstorm development this afternoon. Some surface-based CAPE\non the order of 1-2 kJ/kg is supportive for some strong/severe \nstorms. There\u2019s some modest ~20-30 kt effective shear and \ngenerally less than 700 J/kg of DCAPE. If storms fire this \nafternoon, they will likely be focused in the vicinity of the \ncold front, affecting areas roughly along the US-2 corridor and \nsouth, and maybe up to the Iron Range. Hail looks to be the \nprimary threat, and we could see some quarter size to maybe ping\npong ball size hail in a few storms. Some gusty winds or \nperhaps a very brief tornado can\u2019t be totally ruled out (~2% \nchance) with at least marginally favorable southwesterly winds \nand low-level shear ahead of the cold front, but there does look\nto be a general mismatch where the best shear early this \nafternoon may not coincide with the best instability in the \nlater afternoon and evening such that the tornado risk is low. \nThe strong/severe storms may develop around 3 PM this afternoon \nand linger as they move into northwest Wisconsin through about 9\nor 10 PM. They should weaken pretty quickly after sunset (at \nleast as far as the severe threat goes), though they won\u2019t \ndissipate completely as they move east with the front. After the\nfront, for the rest of the night, isolated pop-up showers can\u2019t\nbe ruled out with a little lift aloft and moisture remaining. \nAn overall drying trend is expected though. \n\nOn Friday, a secondary cold front and cyclonic vorticity \nadvection aloft may trigger some afternoon showers and perhaps \nsome storms. At this point, severe weather isn\u2019t expected with \npretty much straight northwest flow expected, but we could see \njust under 1 kJ/kg of MUCAPE in the afternoon that could support\na few non-severe storms. Otherwise, skies are looking partly\ncloudy in the afternoon after mainly overcast skies during the\nmorning with fog (possibly dense for some areas, especially\naround the head of Lake Superior with a period of northeast\nwinds likely later tonight into Friday morning). Isolated\nshowers could linger into Friday evening.\n\nGoing into the weekend, broad ridging across the western CONUS\nwith surface high pressure over Manitoba/North Dakota will bring\nus some slightly cooler weather with northwest flow. The weather\nis looking very quiet and dry over the weekend. The only main\nconcern will be some lower RH values falling a bit below 40%,\nespecially on Sunday, which could lead to some slightly \nelevated fire weather concerns. But, winds will be pretty light \nSunday/Monday when the driest conditions arrive, so even this is\na small concern.\n\nNext week, another low pressure system may bring some warm air\nadvection-induced showers and storms Monday night into Tuesday.\nA potential low pressure system may take a similar track as\ntoday's low, bringing another cold front through sometime around\nTuesday evening/night.\n\nFor the rest of the week, generally northwest flow aloft should\nkeep us in a quiet weather pattern to end the week.",
